{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 240,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the perhaps not too distant future, every radio telescope on Earth receives the same massive data  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6160,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dump from what can only be an advanced alien species. It seems to contain the detailed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11440,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "instructions for building a faster-than-light \npropulsion device, along with a reference manual  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15920,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "containing what amounts to all physics known by the aliens - including the equations of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20480,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their laws of physics and a list of all elementary particles and their properties and interactions.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25840,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we can correctly decipher this \nstuff, we can build warp-ships  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29520,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and join the galactic civilization. Of course \nif we get it wrong we might blow up the planet. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40320,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How hard can it really be to decode \nalien physics and engineering?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44640,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s gotta map to our own physics - I mean, we live in the same universe after all.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49360,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We start by noticing that the alien technology seems to use good ol’ fashioned electronics,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55440,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even if it is insanely complex. We know \nthis because the particle carried by the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60000,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "alien circuitry looks like the electron. We  decide this through a process of elimination. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65200,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The aliens describe two sets of spin-1/2 \nparticles, each with 3 generations - these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70080,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "must be the quarks and the leptons, which \nare distinguishable from each other by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73600,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their relative masses, color charge, etc. The \nparticle running through the alien circuitry  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79200,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is the lightest lepton and has negative \nelectric charge. Obviously the electron."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85840,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Actually, no. Unbeknownst to us, these aliens use positronic circuitry, not electronic. It’s just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93360,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that they define electric charge in the opposite way - electrons positive, positrons negative.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99680,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But how could we make such a blunder? Surely, a quick glance at the alien Maxwell’s equations  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105120,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which govern the interactions of charged \nparticles will reveal that they use a flipped  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109440,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sign convention. Nope. It turns out that \nnot only is th e sign convention arbitrary,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115520,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but there’s no way to tell which was \nchosen based on the laws of physics alone. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120960,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we build the device with its \nincorrect electronic circuitry.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125120,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The President exerts some pressure \nto switch it on already because  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129120,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an election is coming up. But just before \nwe flip the switch and destroy the planet,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134560,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a few cautious scientists take one last look \nat the alien documentation. So what do you think?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140240,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Is there anything in the way the aliens wrote down their laws of physics that might reveal our error?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145920,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we figure that out, let’s recall how we \nhumans decided on our own electric charge sign  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151760,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "convention. It was a pretty arbitrary choice \nbased on a mistake. Made by this guy, Benjamin  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158960,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Franklin. The nature of electricity was just \none among Franklin’s many scientific interests. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165280,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By Franklin’s time, we already knew that if \nyou rub a glass rod with a piece of cloth,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170640,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "both gain an electric charge. \nFranklin was the first to guess  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174640,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that both rod and cloth gain the same type of charge, just with opposite signs to the charge. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181360,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He imagined an electric fluid that flowed \nfrom one to the other, so that in one you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186480,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have an excess of fluid and in the other \nan absence. Then the direction of the force  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193520,
    "dDurationMs": 8480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "comes from multiplying the signs  of the density of this fluid together \n- bring together two positive charges or   "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202000,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "two negative charges and you get a net positive force - which means a repulsive force by the way. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209920,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a negative and positive charge brought together leads to a negative force - and that is an attractive force. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 216880,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The choice of sign convention actually doesn’t matter as long as you stay consistent. Which  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222640,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is lucky, because the reasoning \nbehind Franklin’s choice was wrong.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227360,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He noticed that when you rub the glass \nrod with a piece of cloth, you can get a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232160,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more powerful shock from the rod compared to the cloth. He imagined the electrical fluid  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238800,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "building up in the rod and flowing into your hand - so he defined the rod's charge as positive. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246320,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We now know that it’s the other way \naround. Electrons from the rod are actually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250560,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rubbed off onto the cloth, so there’s a \nflow of matter in the opposite direction.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255680,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You get a stronger shock from the rod because the charge moves around more easily inside the glass.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262080,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means a large charge difference builds \nup in the rod near your point of contact,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268320,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and that causes electrons to jump \nfrom your hand into the rod. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271520,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This was in the early part of the 1700s. It was a century and a half later before we actually discovered that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277760,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the electron existed. That discovery finally allowed us to figure out which way this “fluid” was really flowing.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284240,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But by then it was too late; what we now call \nthe Franklin convention was fully entrenched.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290000,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Electrons came to be defined as having \nnegative charge, and that defined the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293840,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relative charge of everything else - including \nthe positive charge for protons and positrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299760,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Happily, Benjamin Franklin’s misguided choice doesn’t make any difference to the laws of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304800,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electromagnetism - the Maxwell’s equations. Those equations tell us how particles with   "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electric charges respond to each other. They \ndon’t care what names we give those particles.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314720,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Had Franklin defined charge the other way \naround, Maxwell’s equations would be unchanged."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320480,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this is bad news for our fictional scenario because we can’t rely on the equations describing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327520,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the laws of physics to determine the \nalien sign convention for electric charge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333360,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our problem is compounded by the fact that matter and antimatter seem to be identical except for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 338880,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "having exactly opposite charges. The universe is mostly symmetric under charge conjugation - switch  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346400,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all matter with antimatter and vice versa and the universe would keep ticking along almost exactly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351280,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same. But not quite exactly. It’s clear that the universe itself knows the difference between  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357520,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter and antimatter; after all, it contains \nalmost exclusively the former. And that fact  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363280,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tells us that somewhere buried deep in the laws of physics is a subtle breaking of charge symmetry. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371360,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So in order to save the world from poorly assembled alien technology, we need to find a single  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376240,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "manifestation of this symmetry breaking. The classic example is the decay of the kaon particle,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382560,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which we’ve talked about in detail previously. The long story short is that this unstable  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387200,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "meson decays very differently compared to its antiparticle. So as long as our compendium  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 393600,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of alien physics describes the interactions \nof specific particles, we can distinguish  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 398800,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter from antimatter in the case of kaons. \nThat breaks the frustrating symmetry of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 405040,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entire alien system of physics, allowing \nus to identify its charge sign convention,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410720,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distinguish positrons from electrons, \nand hopefully not explode the planet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 415120,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But maybe we’re not quite there yet. The Franklin convention for the sign of electrically charged  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 420640,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles is not the only arbitrary choice we’ve \nmade in our development of the laws of physics.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426240,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What if alien physics made other choices differently? Let's say that this rather finicky device  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432000,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also cares about the handedness of the positron. Weirdly, there are right-handed and left-handed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437360,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "versions of all particles with quantum spin. \nIf we build the device with positions that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442320,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are spinning the wrong way then, I dunno, \nmaybe we create a black hole that eats the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446560,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "planet. But the definition of handedness \nalso depends on an arbitrary convention,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451520,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just like the sign of electric charge - in this \ncase, something called the right-hand rule."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458320,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s put aside quantum spin for a minute to \ndefine this rule. Say you have an electron moving  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463680,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through a magnetic field. That field is going to apply a force in some direction to that electron.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469840,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To find that direction you just point the index finger of your right hand in the direction the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475920,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electron is moving and your middle finger in the direction of the magnetic field, then your thumb  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481920,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will be pointing in the direction of the force.\n￼ "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484800,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Mathematically we calculate the force using \nsomething called the cross product. Force equals  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490480,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "charge times velocity cross magnetic field. \nThis is a type of vector multiplication where  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 496560,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the direction of the final vector - in this case \nforce - can be found using the right hand rule.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502960,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Cross products are everywhere in physics, for example in calculating torque, angular momentum,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 508320,
    "dDurationMs": 2240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and through Maxwell’s equations. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 510560,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The right hand rule is another arbitrary \nconvention, and its choice also determines how we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515120,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "define particle handedness or partical chirality. What if our aliens were predominantly left-handed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522720,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or left tentacled or whatever? They might \ndefine chirality in the opposite way,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 527600,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with devastating existential \nconsequences. Fortunately,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 532640,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this switch in convention does seem to change the actual equations of the laws of physics. To make  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539200,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a LEFT-hand-rule work you need to either flip the order of the two vectors in the cross product,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544400,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so field cross velocity instead of the other way around, OR you need to add a minus sign in front  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550160,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the equation. So it sounds like we could figure out which convention the aliens used just by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555520,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "looking at their Maxwell’s equations. But actually no - at least not without other information. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 562640,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To understand why, imagine that all right hands in the universe were transformed into left hands and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568640,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vice versa. In other words, imagine reflecting the whole universe in a mirror. We call such  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 573920,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a transformation a parity inversion. Parity is \nanother fundamental and broken symmetry of nature  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580240,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like charge, and just as with charge symmetry, parity is related to an arbitrary convention - the right hand rule. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588000,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact charge and parity are intimately tied. I just told you that you can undo the effects  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593440,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of using the left-hand rule by placing a minus sign in front of your magnetic force equation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598960,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well that minus sign can equally be interpreted as a flip in the sign of the electric charge in that equation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 606400,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you change the convention for parity by using the left-hand-rule AND you change the convention  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 611600,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for charge by flipping its sign then the \ndifferences cancel out and you’re left with the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 616800,
    "dDurationMs": 8208,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same laws of physics. So can we figure out what parity convention our alien benefactors are using?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625008,
    "dDurationMs": 6032,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, only if we also know their sign convention. But if we don’t know that then  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631040,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there’s no way to tell from the \nmaster equations themselves. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635840,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just as with charge, if we dig into the \nway particles interact we can find a clue."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641680,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Parity is the symmetry between left and right handed chirality for particles with quantum spin,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648320,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and in our universe P-symmetry is broken in \nmuch more obvious ways than charge symmetry.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 655120,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s because the Weak Force only interacts with left handed particles and right handed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 660320,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "anti-particles, not at all with right-handed particles nor left-handed antiparticles. If we search the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 667280,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "alien user manual for the part that describes weak force interactions we should be able to figure out  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 673120,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which handed rule the aliens are using. But again, this depends on our ability to distinguish matter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679920,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from anti-matter, so we still need to know which sign convention they use for electric charge. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686480,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The combination of charge and parity \ntransformation - appears to bring our equations  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 691600,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back to where they started. This is CP symmetry. A mirror-reflected, antimatter universe has the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 698560,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same laws of physics as ours. But actually, \nsuch a universe works differently in very,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705520,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very subtle ways. That universe works just like our universe if you reversed the flow of time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712400,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The final symmetry and the final convention that we need to figure out is how the aliens define  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719040,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the flow of time. Do they define the future as an increasing progression of positive times like we do,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725680,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the past as negative? Or do they do it the other way around? The laws of physics shouldn’t care  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732240,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which you choose - so we say those \nlaws are time reversal symmetric.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737840,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if time reversal is equivalent to \ncharge-parity inversion, and the latter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 743360,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is a broken symmetry, then time symmetry \nmust also be broken. The grand, unbroken  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 748560,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "symmetry - CPT symmetry - is a combination of all three. The laws of physics and the behavior  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 755200,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of nature is unchanged under simultaneous inversion of charge, parity and time. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 762000,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And here we might have our \nfinal, insurmountable problem.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 766400,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What if the aliens use reverse conventions for the sign of electric charge, the handedness of parity,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772400,
    "dDurationMs": 7193,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the direction of the flow of time? The change in the time-flow convention along with the changes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779593,
    "dDurationMs": 5367,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in charge and parity would change how they represent their particle interactions in a way that we couldn’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784960,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distinguish. We might build a time-forward, \nright-handed, electronic device instead of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 791040,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the alien’s intended time-reversed, left-handed, positronic doodad. And guess what - that should be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 799440,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "totally fine. The unbroken nature of CPT symmetry means the thing should work just as intended.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806800,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like assembling a bookshelf upside down because you inverted the instruction manual - the thing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 811920,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "still holds books just fine. It may be \nembarrassing when we warp into the galactic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 816880,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "civilization with our spaceship on backwards, but the important thing is we get there."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 822880,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The takeaways from all of this: 1) check if your alien instruction manual is upside down, and 2)  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 829840,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "remember that whenever we measure something, anything, we are making choices. Those choices  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836240,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can seem inconsequential, but every seemingly arbitrary choice reveals something powerful.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 843200,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It points to the existence of an \nunderlying symmetry of space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849520,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thanks to everyone who supports us on \nPatreon. Every little bit really helps  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 853680,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "give us the stability we need to keep making this show. Today’s special Patreon shoutout goes to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 859840,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bryce Fort, who’s supporting us at the big bang level. Bryce, we realised it was selfish to just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 865360,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sit waiting for aliens to send us their advanced technology, so we’ve used your contribution  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871680,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to blast our own radio beam to hundreds of nearby stellar systems. It contains our own confusingly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 878800,
    "dDurationMs": 8046,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "incomplete laws of physics. We actually ran out of space to include a lot of technology. We had to ditch the space shuttle blueprints. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 886846,
    "dDurationMs": 5632,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact we only had just enough bits remaining to ad instructions for making some chocolate brownies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 892478,
    "dDurationMs": 4884,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which some would say are our most advanced achievement as a species anyway. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 897362,
    "dDurationMs": 6185,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the good news is that if they screw it up and accidentally bake time-reversed antimatter brownies"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 903547,
    "dDurationMs": 3614,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they don't blow up their planet. In fact those things are freaking delicious."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 907161,
    "dDurationMs": 4239,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bryce, thanks for helping us enhance the collective wisdom of the galaxy. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 911400,
    "dDurationMs": 1640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": " \nA quick announcement before  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913040,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we get to comments: we’ll be taking a two-week break from releasing episodes. Don’t worry,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 918800,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’ll be making episodes extra hard during that time, and this gap will give us the time we need "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 924160,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to make some extra amazing stuff. So today we’re answering comments for our episode on whether  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 931280,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interstellar travel is even possible for living \nbeings - in particular given the extreme hazards  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 936560,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of traveling at relativistic speeds through \nthe dust and gas that lives between the stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It occurs to Greg N that we’ll most likely \nencounter the machines built by intelligent  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 946720,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "civilizations than the lifeforms themselves. So you know who else that occurred to? One of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 953040,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "greatest savants of 20th century math and physics, John von Neumann. He came up with the concept  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 959920,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the von Neumann probe - a self replicating device designed to propagate across the galaxy.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 965520,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even if interstellar travel is near \nimpossible for living things, the fact  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 969760,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the galaxy could be completely filled with von Neumann probes from a single civilization  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 974560,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a million years or less is a strong factor \nto consider when pondering the Fermi paradox.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 981280,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We did an episode on this once upon a time.\n  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 984080,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Knurlgnar24 reminds us that the thing that’s \neven harder than accelerating to relativistic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 988000,
    "dDurationMs": 7485,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "speeds - and that’s stopping, because the ship itself needs to carry all the energy that it needs to stop. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 995485,
    "dDurationMs": 3635,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Where as the energy to accelerate can be based back on Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 999120,
    "dDurationMs": 6294,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And an example is something like a giant laser array pushing a light sail.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1005414,
    "dDurationMs": 5466,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So this concern is very valid. Slowing down takes as much energy as speeding up.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1010880,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If your destination isn’t already set up \nwith a nice decelerating laser or similar,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1016480,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how do you slow? The problem is worse than that actually. Without an external source of momentum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1022160,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exchange like with a light sail, you need to to carry your entire deceleration reaction mass with you.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1028240,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You need to throw as much stuff ahead of you as you threw behind you in speeding up,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1033200,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and all of that reaction mass needs to be \naccelerated with your ship in the first place.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1037840,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": " \nFortunately there are some ideas for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1040720,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "non-reaction-mass deceleration that don’t require you to have already got to your destination.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1046960,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Examples include ridiculously vast light \nsails that decelerate in the light and wind  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1052400,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and magnetic field of the destination star, \nor Bussard ramjets - vast scoops that collect  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1058720,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "diffuse interstellar material as reaction \nmass and fusion fuel for the slowdown phase.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1065760,
    "dDurationMs": 960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Actually Tony Tee  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1066720,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seems to have independently invented  \nthe Bussard ramjet, by the way. Shame you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1070800,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "didn’t get to it 60 years earlier, Tony \n- the Tee Jet has a nice ring to it.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1075440,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": " Regarding shielding against interstellar material,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1079600,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Banoffey asks whether an electromagnetic shield would be sufficient. Well actually a magnetic shield may be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1087040,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enough to block many charged particles, but \nneutral particles like dust would pass right  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1092240,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through. There are proposals for ionizing these particles before they hit your magnetic shield,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1097760,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or for generating a hot plasma in that magnetic field to vaporize particles. There are lots of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1103840,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "novel shielding ideas that we could’t survey in this episode - the point was really to assess  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1109520,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whether interstellar travel was a fundamental no-go, which it seems to NOT be the case. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1116240,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": " \nCessationoftime has a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1117920,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "beautiful vision of a galactic “humanity” in which we’ve spliced our DNA with that of the the naked  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1123040,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mole rat and the tardigrade to grant us radiation resistance for these long interstellar journeys.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1128560,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So I knew about tardigrades, but thanks for sending me down the naked mole rat rabbithole. Those  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1133280,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "things are amazing and I’m completely on board with joining genetic forces. With luck we’ll also  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1138960,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inherit other traits, like proportional longevity, hibernation in extreme low-oxygen environments,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1145440,
    "dDurationMs": 5829,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the ability to absorb parenting \nhormones through eating the queen moles feces. "
    } ]
  } ]
}
